<main id="main">
<section class="phero"><div class="phero-bg"><img src="/assets/img/photos/kitchen.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/kitchen-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/kitchen.webp 1024w" sizes="100vw" width="1024" height="683" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" data-parallax="0.15"></div><div class="wrap phero-in"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><i aria-hidden="true">›</i><span aria-current="page">Claim FAQ</span></nav><p class="kicker">Answers by topic</p><h1>Kansas Insurance Claim and Appraisal FAQ</h1><p class="lede">Plain answers grouped by topic: appraisal basics, Kansas specifics, cost and timing, and what happens after the award.</p><div class="btn-row"><a class="btn btn-gold" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1.9.4 1.8.7 2.7a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8 9.8a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.3a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.4c.9.3 1.8.6 2.7.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2z"/></svg> Call (844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn btn-line" href="/request-kansas-claim-review/">Free claim review</a></div></div></section>
<div class="wrap layout"><article class="prose"><div class="note"><b class="h">Topics on this page</b><ul class="chips"><li>Appraisal basics</li><li>Kansas specifics</li><li>Cost and timing</li><li>After the award</li></ul></div><h2>Appraisal basics</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary><span>What is insurance appraisal?</span><svg class="pl" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M12 5v14M5 12h14"/></svg></summary><div class="a"><p>A process written into most property policies for settling disagreements about the amount of a covered loss. Each side names an appraiser, the appraisers pick an umpire, and two of the three signatures set the amount.</p></div></details><details><summary><span>Is appraisal the same as arbitration?</span><svg class="pl" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M12 5v14M5 12h14"/></svg></summary><div class="a"><p>Not quite. Appraisal is limited to the amount of loss and is usually less formal. Arbitration can address broader issues, depending on the agreement.</p></div></details><details><summary><span>Who can demand appraisal?</span><svg class="pl" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M12 5v14M5 12h14"/></svg></summary><div class="a"><p>Either you or the insurer, if the policy contains an appraisal clause and the parties disagree on the amount of loss.</p></div></details><details><summary><span>Does appraisal decide coverage?</span><svg class="pl" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M12 5v14M5 12h14"/></svg></summary><div class="a"><p>Generally no. If the insurer says something isn’t covered, that is a coverage question for the insurer, the Kansas Insurance Department or an attorney.</p></div></details></div><h2>Kansas specifics</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary><span>Who regulates insurers in Kansas?</span><svg class="pl" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M12 5v14M5 12h14"/></svg></summary><div class="a"><p>The Kansas Insurance Department, led by the elected Commissioner of Insurance. It offers consumer assistance and takes complaints about insurers.</p></div></details><details><summary><span>Does Kansas have a FAIR Plan?</span><svg class="pl" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M12 5v14M5 12h14"/></svg></summary><div class="a"><p>Yes. The Kansas FAIR Plan provides basic property insurance to owners who can’t get coverage in the voluntary market. Eligibility rules apply, including prior declinations.</p></div></details><details><summary><span>What happens if my insurer handles a claim unfairly?</span><svg class="pl" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M12 5v14M5 12h14"/></svg></summary><div class="a"><p>Kansas has an unfair claim settlement practices law enforced by the Insurance Department, and statutes such as K.S.A. 40-256 and 40-908 can allow attorney fees in some lawsuits against insurers. A Kansas attorney can tell you whether any of that fits your situation.</p></div></details><details><summary><span>Are percentage hail deductibles common in Kansas?</span><svg class="pl" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M12 5v14M5 12h14"/></svg></summary><div class="a"><p>Yes. Many Kansas homeowner policies carry a wind/hail deductible set as a percentage of the dwelling limit. Check your declarations page.</p></div></details><details><summary><span>What is a roof payment schedule?</span><svg class="pl" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M12 5v14M5 12h14"/></svg></summary><div class="a"><p>An endorsement that pays a percentage of a roof’s replacement cost based on its age. It is increasingly common on policies sold in hail-prone states.</p></div></details></div><h2>Cost and timing</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary><span>What does appraisal cost?</span><svg class="pl" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M12 5v14M5 12h14"/></svg></summary><div class="a"><p>You pay your appraiser and usually half the umpire’s fee. Arrangements vary, so get the fee in writing before work starts.</p></div></details><details><summary><span>How long does it take?</span><svg class="pl" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M12 5v14M5 12h14"/></svg></summary><div class="a"><p>Simple residential claims often resolve in six to ten weeks after both appraisers are named. Complex or commercial claims take longer.</p></div></details><details><summary><span>Is it worth it for a small claim?</span><svg class="pl" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M12 5v14M5 12h14"/></svg></summary><div class="a"><p>Often not. If the gap is small relative to fees, a documented supplement request is usually better. Our appraisal payoff projector helps you check.</p></div></details><details><summary><span>Do I have to repair before appraisal?</span><svg class="pl" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M12 5v14M5 12h14"/></svg></summary><div class="a"><p>No. But temporary repairs to prevent further damage are usually required, and recoverable depreciation is generally paid after repairs.</p></div></details></div><h2>After the award</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary><span>Is the award binding?</span><svg class="pl" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M12 5v14M5 12h14"/></svg></summary><div class="a"><p>On the amount of loss, an award signed by two of the three panel members is generally binding, subject to limited challenges.</p></div></details><details><summary><span>When will I be paid?</span><svg class="pl" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M12 5v14M5 12h14"/></svg></summary><div class="a"><p>The policy sets the payment timing after an award. Deductibles, prior payments and recoverable depreciation rules still apply.</p></div></details><details><summary><span>What if the insurer won’t pay the award?</span><svg class="pl" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M12 5v14M5 12h14"/></svg></summary><div class="a"><p>That is a matter for a Kansas attorney. The award is strong evidence of the amount owed.</p></div></details><details><summary><span>Does the roof schedule still apply after an award?</span><svg class="pl" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M12 5v14M5 12h14"/></svg></summary><div class="a"><p>Usually yes. The award sets the roof’s full replacement cost; the schedule then determines what percentage is paid.</p></div></details></div></article>
<aside class="aside"><div class="side-box side-call"><h2 class="side-t">Talk it through, free</h2><p>A short first call. We’ll tell you honestly whether appraisal is worth it.</p><a class="side-phone" href="tel:+18445371427">+1 (844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn btn-gold btn-block" href="/request-kansas-claim-review/">Send claim details</a></div><div class="side-box"><h2 class="side-t">Resources</h2><ul class="side-list"><li><a href="/kansas-appraisal-process/">Kansas appraisal process</a></li><li><a href="/kansas-claim-faq/" aria-current="page">Claim FAQ</a></li><li><a href="/kansas-claim-glossary/">Claim glossary</a></li><li><a href="/kansas-appraisal-guides/">Appraisal guides</a></li></ul></div></aside></div><section class="cta-strip"><div class="wrap cta-strip-in rv"><div><h2>Didn’t see your question?</h2><p>Call or email. We’re happy to talk it through.</p></div><div class="btn-row"><a class="btn btn-gold" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1.9.4 1.8.7 2.7a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8 9.8a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.3a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.4c.9.3 1.8.6 2.7.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2z"/></svg> (844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn btn-line" href="/request-kansas-claim-review/">Email the estimate</a></div></div></section>
